#{["Given a fixed **origin** in the plane (often denoted ![](images/75/7502527c010624a805906c12d7a6e94363988e41b1c40771a4cc4fc82900e083.svg)), and another point ![](images/42/42193b6382254991fb9c053ead9edd0631767155ea51d8a8551cfc4fc734340e.svg) the vector ![](images/20/20edf7e26e0b35148bf8ff1e9b52c38e5b6672ae0462a8c2e218e1c0456bae07.svg) is called the **position vector** of the point ![](images/ed/ed6dc1fd057e3dd2207c735a562bf4db22b72d1a9380651607439f00c0440725.svg)\n\nSo, the position vector tells us how to move from the origin to the point.\n\nFor example, suppose ![](images/4f/4f9204dcee76e03f03dc64827bf810327c9b2b981a0aee5a10c7f80ca98f4d5d.svg) and ![](images/41/412bd151cdf5602575028591e2f87d9a7e0328aa6b18dccb6459e7041a5d9b9c.svg) How can we find the position vector ![](images/20/20edf7e26e0b35148bf8ff1e9b52c38e5b6672ae0462a8c2e218e1c0456bae07.svg) in terms of ![](images/52/527e0abeb684dd24d8c92e45425542080c4c9493b9460bb89753fef3226c1f92.svg) and ![](images/c5/c5d2e1827ba6a11504655381514c15ce7eba142e62bf8dca3a10d12eef9d7861.svg)\n\n![](images/e6/e61003fb62534b04cfbbac07a64e61f6c16e2d464eb35bc5b98654b0603b24db.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we can trace a path from ![](images/75/7502527c010624a805906c12d7a6e94363988e41b1c40771a4cc4fc82900e083.svg) to ![](images/46/46833dd7644e5c60bb4525fe4e7649a99752b26096331755a4b44c2abe183f11.svg) by first moving from ![](images/75/7502527c010624a805906c12d7a6e94363988e41b1c40771a4cc4fc82900e083.svg) to ![](images/c2/c2c85081d1f89d74b8ebb2761496d211d7052193809b7864e7983437f5d0b80f.svg) and then from ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) to ![](images/ed/ed6dc1fd057e3dd2207c735a562bf4db22b72d1a9380651607439f00c0440725.svg) Therefore,\n![](images/ac/ac96d55fd0d74cf5fd24f1f1a96beb3865bc70bc45d17be5aad209c5adb1be6d.svg)\n\nSince ![](images/4f/4f9204dcee76e03f03dc64827bf810327c9b2b981a0aee5a10c7f80ca98f4d5d.svg) and ![](images/e2/e283cb6e7ab325c89dd652abd93ff1382b714462887d8839dfed8769693cc7a7.svg) we get\n![](images/e1/e1bde038ef63f77c3ecc5357b49999c96dd6d331aa7455fcdd1935b1f3eb8261.svg)\n\nSo, to find a position vector, we can trace a convenient path from the origin to the point and add the vectors along that path." #edb/ref 13194139538976 #edb/ref 17592186576411 false] ["Given a fixed **origin** in the plane (often denoted ![](images/75/7502527c010624a805906c12d7a6e94363988e41b1c40771a4cc4fc82900e083.svg)), and another point ![](images/42/42193b6382254991fb9c053ead9edd0631767155ea51d8a8551cfc4fc734340e.svg) the vector ![](images/20/20edf7e26e0b35148bf8ff1e9b52c38e5b6672ae0462a8c2e218e1c0456bae07.svg) is called the **position vector** of the point ![](images/ed/ed6dc1fd057e3dd2207c735a562bf4db22b72d1a9380651607439f00c0440725.svg)\n\nSo, the position vector tells us how to move from the origin to the point.\n\nFor example, suppose ![](images/4f/4f9204dcee76e03f03dc64827bf810327c9b2b981a0aee5a10c7f80ca98f4d5d.svg) and ![](images/41/412bd151cdf5602575028591e2f87d9a7e0328aa6b18dccb6459e7041a5d9b9c.svg) How can we find the position vector ![](images/20/20edf7e26e0b35148bf8ff1e9b52c38e5b6672ae0462a8c2e218e1c0456bae07.svg) in terms of ![](images/52/527e0abeb684dd24d8c92e45425542080c4c9493b9460bb89753fef3226c1f92.svg) and ![](images/c5/c5d2e1827ba6a11504655381514c15ce7eba142e62bf8dca3a10d12eef9d7861.svg)\n\n![](images/e6/e61003fb62534b04cfbbac07a64e61f6c16e2d464eb35bc5b98654b0603b24db.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we can trace a path from ![](images/75/7502527c010624a805906c12d7a6e94363988e41b1c40771a4cc4fc82900e083.svg) to ![](images/46/46833dd7644e5c60bb4525fe4e7649a99752b26096331755a4b44c2abe183f11.svg) by first moving from ![](images/75/7502527c010624a805906c12d7a6e94363988e41b1c40771a4cc4fc82900e083.svg) to ![](images/c2/c2c85081d1f89d74b8ebb2761496d211d7052193809b7864e7983437f5d0b80f.svg) and then from ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) to ![](images/ed/ed6dc1fd057e3dd2207c735a562bf4db22b72d1a9380651607439f00c0440725.svg) Therefore,\n![](images/ac/ac96d55fd0d74cf5fd24f1f1a96beb3865bc70bc45d17be5aad209c5adb1be6d.svg)\n\nSince ![](images/4f/4f9204dcee76e03f03dc64827bf810327c9b2b981a0aee5a10c7f80ca98f4d5d.svg) and ![](images/e2/e283cb6e7ab325c89dd652abd93ff1382b714462887d8839dfed8769693cc7a7.svg) we get\n![](images/e1/e1bde038ef63f77c3ecc5357b49999c96dd6d331aa7455fcdd1935b1f3eb8261.svg)\n\nSo, to find a position vector, we can trace a convenient path from the origin to the point and add the vectors along that path." #edb/ref 13194139533438 #edb/ref 17592186045417 true] ["Given a fixed **origin** in the plane (often denoted $O$), and another point $X$, the vector $\\overrightarrow{OX}$ is called the **position vector** of the point $X$. The vector $\\mathbf{x}$ defines the position of $X$ on the plane in relation to $O$.\n\nFor example, suppose we have two vectors\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf{a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OB}=\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nand a parallelogram $OAXB_1$, where\n\n$$\n{OB_1}=2{OB}\n$$\n\nHow can we find the vector $\\mathbf{x}$, the diagonal of the parallelogram, expressed in terms of $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$?\n\n![](images/70/707f54e69e47d391432fe6a15e92fab108d4f698dc13c0b04c8c392b72a5386b.png)\n\nFrom the $\\triangle OB_1X$ in the above figure, we can see that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = \\overrightarrow{OB}_1+\\overrightarrow{B_1X}\n$$\n\nWe know that\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OB}_1=2\\overrightarrow{OB}=2\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nAlso, since $OAXB_1$ is a parallelogram, we have $OA \\parallel B_1X$ and\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{B_1X}=\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf{a}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the original equality, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= OB^(→)_{1} + B_{1}X^(→) \\\\\n&= 2b + a \\\\\n&= a + 2b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533438 #edb/ref 17592186045417 false] ["Given a fixed **origin** in the plane (often denoted $O$), and another point $X$, the vector $\\overrightarrow{OX}$ is called the **position vector** of the point $X$. The vector $\\mathbf{x}$ defines the position of $X$ on the plane in relation to $O$.\n\nFor example, suppose we have two vectors\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf{a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OB}=\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nand a parallelogram $OAXB_1$, where\n\n$$\n{OB_1}=2{OB}\n$$\n\nHow can we find the vector $\\mathbf{x}$, the diagonal of the parallelogram, expressed in terms of $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$?\n\n![](images/70/707f54e69e47d391432fe6a15e92fab108d4f698dc13c0b04c8c392b72a5386b.png)\n\nFrom the $\\triangle OB_1X$ in the above figure, we can see that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = \\overrightarrow{OB}_1+\\overrightarrow{B_1X}\n$$\n\nWe know that\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OB}_1=2\\overrightarrow{OB}=2\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nAlso, since $OAXB_1$ is a parallelogram, we have $OA \\parallel B_1X$ and\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{B_1X}=\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf{a}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the original equality, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= OB^(→)_{1} + B_{1}X^(→) \\\\\n&= 2b + a \\\\\n&= a + 2b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533355 #edb/ref 17592186045417 true] ["Given a fixed **origin** in the plane (often denoted $O$), and another point $X,$ the vector $\\overrightarrow{OX}$ is called the **position vector** of the point $X.$\n\nSo, the position vector tells us how to move from the origin to the point.\n\nFor example, suppose $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\overset{\\to -}{A X} = \\mathbf{b} .$ How can we find the position vector $\\overrightarrow{OX}$ in terms of $\\mathbf a$ and $\\mathbf{b}?$\n\n![](images/e6/e61003fb62534b04cfbbac07a64e61f6c16e2d464eb35bc5b98654b0603b24db.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we can trace a path from $O$ to $X$ by first moving from $O$ to $A,$ and then from $A$ to $X.$ Therefore,\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OX} = \\overrightarrow{OA}+\\overrightarrow{AX}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\overset{\\to -}{A X} = \\mathbf{b} ,$ we get\n$$\n\\overset{\\to -}{O X} = \\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b} .\n$$\n\nSo, to find a position vector, we can trace a convenient path from the origin to the point and add the vectors along that path." #edb/ref 13194139538976 #edb/ref 17592186576411 true]}
